@document.meta
title: Tuning the search ranker
slug: search-ranking
date: 2026-05-15
summary: Why the in-memory ranker weighs title, tags, summary, and body differently, and where it still gets results wrong.
tags: [search, ranking, devlog]
draft: false
@end

Search runs on every Ctrl+K keystroke that settles. It has no database and no Elasticsearch behind it: just a slice of `SearchDoc` values and a scorer that walks them.

** The weights
@table
| Field | Weight |
| - | - |
| title | 50 |
| tags | 30 |
| summary | 20 |
| body | 10 |
@end

Title hits dominate on purpose. If you search /norg/ and a post has "Norg" in its title, you almost certainly want that post, ahead of five others that mention the word once.

** The scorer
Each field gets lowercased once per document, then every query token checks it.

@code go
func scoreDoc(doc content.SearchDoc, tokens []string) int {
    title := strings.ToLower(doc.Title)
    summary := strings.ToLower(doc.Summary)
    body := strings.ToLower(doc.Body)
    tags := strings.ToLower(strings.Join(doc.Tags, " "))

    score := 0
    for _, token := range tokens {
        if strings.Contains(title, token) {
            score += 50
        }
        if strings.Contains(tags, token) {
            score += 30
        }
        if strings.Contains(summary, token) {
            score += 20
        }
        if strings.Contains(body, token) {
            score += 10
        }
    }
    return score
}
@end

Ties go to the newer document.

** Where it falls short
*** DONE type filter prefix (`blog ...`, `projects ...`)
*** DONE parent slug so devlog results link to `/projects/{parent}/{slug}`
*** DONE query cap: anything over 256 bytes gets a `400` (added in July)
*** TODO phrase matching, so `bento grid` counts as one phrase
*** TODO stemming, so /rebuild/ matches /rebuilt/
*** TODO a cap on tag boosts, so a post tagged with the same word five times cannot run away

Phrase matching comes first. The indexer splits the query on whitespace and scores each token alone, so `bento grid` ranks anything that contains /bento/ or /grid/, even when the two words never meet.

A scorer this small is easy to read and just as easy to get wrong. I check it against real queries before I trust the numbers.
